.op
                         ASCWM and WMASC

Microsoft's Basic-80 language provides a facility for breaking up 
a  logicial  line of source code into several physical  lines  by 
using  line-feed or ^J.   This inserts a LFCR (line-feed/carrige-
return)  sequence  into the file as opposed to  the  normal  CRLF 
sequence used to end a statement (and ASCII records).

Using  line-feeds  and tabs one can code BASIC programs that  are 
more pleasing to read,  easier to debug and more self-documenting 
then  by simply allowing the program lines to 'wrap' around  from 
the right side of the screen to the left side.

Example -

100 IF NUMBER%=VALUE%
          THEN VALUE%=NEXT.NUMBER% :GOTO 1234
          ELSE IF NUMBER%=OTHER.NUMBER%
                    THEN VALUE%=0
                    ELSE GOTO 5678           'Check values

The  problem with using this technique is that most printers  and 
full-screen  video  editors  cannot  handle  the  LFCR   sequence 
correctly.   BPSXREF  solves  the  printer problem  and  provides 
facilities for further formatting of the program listing.

Included  with  BPSXREF are two utilities that can be of  use  in 
conjunction  with  a full-screen editor (such  as  WordMaster  or 
MINCE).

The program ASCWM simply replaces all occurences of LFCR with the 
backslash  (\) character and a CRLF sequence.   Input is expected 
to  be a BASIC ASCII file with an extension of '.ASC' as  created 
by  the BASIC-80 interpreter SAVE command using the  'A'  option.  

E.G.   SAVE "PROGNAME.ASC",A

This is the format required by Microsoft's BASIC compiler BASCOM.  
ASCWM will output a new file with an extension of '.WM'.  Editors 
will  have no problems with this file.   Use a backslash and  the 
return  key (\CR) instead of the line-feed key when editing these 
files.

WMASC  reverses  the process and replaces all backslash (\)  CRLF 
sequences  with a LFCR sequence.   The input file is expected  to 
have  an  extension  of '.WM' and the output file  will  have  an 
extension of '.ASC'.   Incidentally WMASC can be used to help  in 
the  conversion  of  CBASIC2 programs to BASIC-80 as  the  CBASIC 
language uses the backslash(\) to split logical lines.

These programs are not copyrighted.   Also, backslash is produced 
by ^9 (cntrl-9) on the TRS-80 Model II.
